WHY: the rule takes the WORST of the gated dimensions, so a fixed cut silently changes meaning as the suite changes. Measured: the same z<=-2 is a 3.7% false-alarm rule on the 15-task suite and 12.9% on the 27-task one. FIRST I measured whether the human's own preferred purchase works: doubling runs per arm does NOT lower the false-alarm rate (new suite 16.0% at n=1/arm, 17.1% at n=2; old suite 5.0% and 3.7%), because under the null a two-proportion z is about N(0,1) at any n. Runs buy POWER, never false-alarm control — said before spending a token, and the human then chose the rule change.
THE RULE: reject when the worst gated z falls at or below the SOFTEST value whose false-alarm rate on that suite's own measured null is still <= 5%. tasks5: -2.46 at 2.80% (a fixed -2 would have been 12.85%). tasks4: -1.44 at 3.71% — IDENTICAL to what the fixed -2 selected there, because the atom gap between -1.44 and -2.13 means both cuts pick the same splits. So the new rule reproduces the old one exactly where the old one was right and fixes it where it drifted. Tool: benchmark/local/harness/calibrate_rule.py, three seeds, identical cut on all three for both suites.
A TRAP I WALKED INTO AND RECORDED: the naive "5th percentile of the null" is WRONG on a discrete null — the index lands inside an atom and rejecting at "<= that atom" takes the whole atom. Measured on tasks4 it turns a 3.7% rule into a 13.4% one. The cut is found by walking from the deepest atom upward instead, and the achieved rate therefore lands under the target rather than on it.
DROPPED in the same change, and this is a removal not a recalibration: the "two or more gated dimensions at -1.5" clause. It never fired ALONE in 60,000 splits of the old null; its only supporting real example evaporated when the probes were repaired; and on the new suite it false-alarms at 9.6% by itself, which no calibration of the primary cut can offset. Covered by the same explicit consent, stated loudly in benchmark/README.md so it can be vetoed.
